## N+1 in Code

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // Lazy by default
    private User user;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Item> items;
}

@Transactional
public List<OrderDTO> getAllOrders() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();  // Query 1: SELECT * FROM orders
    // orders.size() = 100

    return orders.stream()
        .map(order -> {
            return new OrderDTO(
                order.getId(),
                order.getUser().getName(),     // Query 2-101: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?
                order.getItems().size()        // Queries 102-201: SELECT * FROM items WHERE order_id = ?
            );
        })
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
    // Total queries: 1 + 100 + 100 = 201
}
```

## N+1 in JSON Serialization (OSIV)

```java
// With OSIV enabled (spring.jpa.open-in-view=true)
@GetMapping("/orders")
public List<Order> getOrders() {
    return orderRepo.findAll();  // Loads 100 orders
    // Jackson serializes to JSON:
    // - Accesses order.user → N queries!
    // - Accesses order.items → N more queries!
}
```

## Detecting N+1

Enable SQL logging:
```properties
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
```

Or use Hibernate Statistics:
```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true
logging.level.org.hibernate.stat=DEBUG
```

Or use a query counter test:
```java
@Test
void shouldNotCauseNPlusOne() {
    // With datasource-proxy or p6spy, count queries
    AtomicInteger queryCount = new AtomicInteger();
    // Assert queryCount.get() == 1 after getAllOrders()
}
```

## Production Reality

N+1 is one of the most common performance bugs in production Spring applications. An endpoint that works fine with a test dataset of 10 records can become 100x slower in production with 1,000 records. Always review any code that iterates a collection and accesses lazy associations — that pattern is a red flag for N+1.

## Basic JOIN FETCH

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Without JOIN FETCH: N+1 when accessing order.user
    List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);

    // With JOIN FETCH: one query with JOIN
    @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.user WHERE o.status = :status")
    List<Order> findByStatusWithUser(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);

    // Multiple JOIN FETCH
    @Query("SELECT DISTINCT o FROM Order o "
         + "JOIN FETCH o.user "
         + "JOIN FETCH o.items "
         + "WHERE o.id = :id")
    Optional<Order> findByIdWithAll(@Param("id") Long id);
}
```

## Generated SQL

```sql
-- Without JOIN FETCH:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';  -- 1 query
-- Then for each of N orders:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?;              -- N queries

-- With JOIN FETCH:
SELECT DISTINCT o.*, u.*
FROM orders o
INNER JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id
WHERE o.status = 'PENDING';                    -- 1 query
```

## Pagination + JOIN FETCH Problem

```java
// PROBLEMATIC: HHH90003004 warning
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.status = :status")
Page<Order> findWithItemsPaged(@Param("status") OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);
// Hibernate: "HHH90003004: firstResult/maxResults specified with collection fetch"
// → Loads ALL rows in memory, paginates in Java — not DB pagination!
```

## Solution: Two-Query Approach for Pagination

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Page<OrderDTO> findOrdersWithItems(OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable) {
    // Query 1: Paginated IDs only — no JOIN FETCH
    Page<Long> orderIds = orderRepository.findIdsByStatus(status, pageable);

    // Query 2: Fetch full data for this page using IN clause
    List<Order> orders = orderRepository.findByIdInWithItems(orderIds.getContent());

    // Map and return
    List<OrderDTO> dtos = orders.stream().map(orderMapper::toDTO).collect(toList());
    return new PageImpl<>(dtos, pageable, orderIds.getTotalElements());
}

// In repository:
@Query("SELECT o.id FROM Order o WHERE o.status = :status")
Page<Long> findIdsByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);

@Query("SELECT DISTINCT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.id IN :ids")
List<Order> findByIdInWithItems(@Param("ids") List<Long> ids);
```

## DISTINCT with Collection JOIN FETCH

```java
// Without DISTINCT: duplicate Order rows if multiple items
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items")
// Returns: [order1, order1, order1, order2, order2] (one per item)

// With DISTINCT: deduplicated
@Query("SELECT DISTINCT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items")
// Returns: [order1, order2]
```

## Production Reality

JOIN FETCH is the primary tool for N+1 elimination, but don't apply it blindly everywhere. Only fetch associations you actually need for a given use case. Eagerly loading everything into every query creates oversized result sets. Profile your specific queries and add JOIN FETCH only where N+1 is causing measurable slowness.

## @BatchSize on Collection

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @BatchSize(size = 30)  // Load up to 30 collections at once
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();
}

// Loading 100 orders and accessing all items:
// Without BatchSize: 101 queries (1 + 100)
// With @BatchSize(30): 1 + ceil(100/30) = 1 + 4 = 5 queries
```

## @BatchSize on ManyToOne

```java
@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @BatchSize(size = 20)  // Load up to 20 proxies at once
    private Category category;
}
```

## Global Setting (Recommended)

```properties
# application.properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=30

# This applies to ALL lazy associations as the default
# Individual @BatchSize annotations override this
```

## Generated SQL with Batch Fetch

```sql
-- Loading 45 orders, accessing items with BatchSize(30)
-- Batch 1: first 30 orders' items
SELECT * FROM items WHERE order_id IN (1, 2, 3, ..., 30);

-- Batch 2: next 15 orders' items
SELECT * FROM items WHERE order_id IN (31, 32, ..., 45);

-- Total: 2 queries instead of 45
```

## JOIN FETCH vs Batch Fetch

| | JOIN FETCH | Batch Fetch |
|---|---|---|
| Query count | Always 1 | ceil(N/batchSize) |
| Pagination safe | No (for collections) | Yes |
| Always loads | Yes | Only when accessed |
| Configuration | Per query | Per annotation / global |
| Best for | Known associations needed | Sometimes-needed associations |

## Production Reality

Set `hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=30` globally — it's a safe default that dramatically reduces N+1 for any lazy associations your code accesses without fully solving it with JOIN FETCH. Then use JOIN FETCH for query-specific cases where you know you always need the association. These two approaches are complementary, not mutually exclusive.

## @NamedEntityGraph on Entity

```java
@Entity
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.withUserAndItems",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("user"),
        @NamedAttributeNode("items")
    }
)
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.withUser",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("user")
    }
)
public class Order {
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private User user;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

## @EntityGraph in Repository

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Apply named graph
    @EntityGraph("Order.withUserAndItems")
    Optional<Order> findById(Long id);

    // Inline attribute paths (alternative to named graph)
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "items"})
    List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);

    // Only load user — not items (different graph)
    @EntityGraph("Order.withUser")
    Page<Order> findByCreatedAtAfter(LocalDateTime since, Pageable pageable);

    // Without entity graph — fully lazy (default)
    List<Order> findByUserId(Long userId);
}
```

## Subgraph for Nested Associations

```java
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.full",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("items"),
        @NamedAttributeNode(value = "user", subgraph = "user-with-address")
    },
    subgraphs = {
        @NamedSubgraph(
            name = "user-with-address",
            attributeNodes = @NamedAttributeNode("address")
        )
    }
)
// Loads: order → items + user → address (3 levels, 1-2 queries)
```

## Entity Graph vs JOIN FETCH

```java
// Entity Graph: works with derived queries, no JPQL needed
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "user")
List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);

// JOIN FETCH: requires JPQL, more explicit
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.user WHERE o.status = :status")
List<Order> findByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);

// Both produce the same SQL — choose based on readability preference
```

## Production Reality

Entity graphs are cleaner for simple association loading without `@Query`. Use them on Spring Data's auto-generated methods. Switch to explicit `@Query` with JOIN FETCH when you need complex WHERE clauses or multiple levels of joins — JPQL gives you more control. For the most critical queries, verify the generated SQL by enabling `show-sql=true` regardless of which approach you use.

## Method 1: SQL Logging (Development)

```properties
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true
logging.level.org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind=TRACE
```

Look for repeated patterns:
```sql
-- N+1 pattern in logs:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;
  ← same query N times!
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 2;
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 3;
...
```

## Method 2: Hibernate Statistics

```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true
logging.level.org.hibernate.stat=DEBUG
```

Output:
```
Session Metrics {
  1045892 nanoseconds spent acquiring 1 JDBC connections;
  14 queries executed to database;
  14 JDBC statements prepared;
  0 JDBC batch statements executed;
  12 entities loaded;
  6 collections loaded;
}
```

## Method 3: datasource-proxy in Tests

```java
@Configuration
@Profile("test")
public class ProxyDataSourceConfig {
    @Bean
    @Primary
    public DataSource proxyDataSource(DataSource originalDataSource) {
        return ProxyDataSourceBuilder.create(originalDataSource)
            .countQuery()
            .build();
    }
}

@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceNPlusOneTest {

    @Autowired
    private OrderService orderService;

    @Autowired
    private DataSource dataSource;  // Actually ProxyDataSource

    @Test
    void shouldNotCauseNPlusOne() {
        QueryCountHolder.clear();

        orderService.getAllOrders();

        QueryCount count = QueryCountHolder.getGrandTotal();
        assertThat(count.getSelect()).isEqualTo(2);  // 1 + 1 batch, not 1 + N
    }
}
```

## Method 4: Micrometer Metrics

```java
@Configuration
public class HibernateMetricsConfig {
    @Bean
    public HibernateMetricsAutoConfiguration hibernateMetrics(
            EntityManagerFactory emf, MeterRegistry registry) {
        return new HibernateMetricsAutoConfiguration();
    }
}

// Grafana query to detect N+1:
// rate(hibernate_sessions_open_total[1m]) / rate(http_server_requests_total[1m])
// High ratio = many sessions per request = potential N+1
```

## Production Reality

Write N+1 detection tests for every endpoint that loads collections. Make it a code review checklist item: every `@OneToMany` access in a loop gets a test assertion on query count. Catching N+1 in CI is infinitely cheaper than diagnosing it in production at 3am when the traffic spike hits.

## DTO Projection Eliminates N+1

```java
// PROBLEM: loading entities causes N+1 for user
List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();
return orders.stream()
    .map(o -> new OrderListDTO(
        o.getId(),
        o.getStatus(),
        o.getUser().getName()   // N queries here!
    ))
    .collect(toList());

// SOLUTION: DTO projection fetches all in one query
public record OrderListDTO(Long id, OrderStatus status, String userName) { }

@Query("SELECT new com.example.dto.OrderListDTO(o.id, o.status, u.name) "
     + "FROM Order o JOIN o.user u "
     + "WHERE o.status = :status")
List<OrderListDTO> findOrderListByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);

// Generated SQL (ONE query):
// SELECT o.id, o.status, u.name
// FROM orders o
// INNER JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id
// WHERE o.status = ?
```

## Interface Projection

```java
public interface OrderListView {
    Long getId();
    String getStatus();
    String getUserName();   // Nested association via method name convention
    UserView getUser();

    interface UserView {
        String getName();
        String getEmail();
    }
}

@Query("SELECT o.id as id, o.status as status, u.name as userName, "
     + "u.email as userEmail FROM Order o JOIN o.user u")
List<OrderListView> findAllAsViews();
// Spring Data generates the proxy — no manual DTO class needed
```

## Aggregation Queries with Projections

```java
public record OrderStatsDTO(Long userId, Long orderCount, BigDecimal totalAmount) { }

@Query("SELECT new com.example.dto.OrderStatsDTO("
     + "o.user.id, COUNT(o), SUM(o.totalAmount)) "
     + "FROM Order o "
     + "GROUP BY o.user.id "
     + "HAVING COUNT(o) > :minOrders")
List<OrderStatsDTO> findUserOrderStats(@Param("minOrders") long minOrders);
// Aggregation in DB — zero N+1 possible
```

## Production Reality

For list/search API endpoints, prefer DTO projections over entity loading. Entities are appropriate for write operations (dirty checking) and detail views (full data). For lists showing 5-10 fields from multiple tables, a DTO projection is faster, avoids N+1 by construction, and uses less memory. This is a clean architectural separation: entities for writes, projections for reads.

## @Fetch(SUBSELECT)

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)  // Hibernate-specific annotation
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

## Generated SQL

```sql
-- Query 1: Load all orders
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';
-- Returns 100 orders

-- When ANY order's items are accessed — loads ALL in one subselect:
SELECT * FROM items
WHERE order_id IN (
  SELECT id FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING'  -- Reuses original criteria!
);
-- Total: 2 queries regardless of N
```

## Strategy Comparison

| | SELECT (default N+1) | BATCH(30) | SUBSELECT | JOIN FETCH |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Queries | 1 + N | 1 + ceil(N/30) | 2 | 1 |
| When triggered | Per access | Per access (batched) | On first access | Never (always eager) |
| Loads all? | No | In batches | Yes | Yes |
| Pagination safe | N/A | Yes | Yes | No |

## Annotation Details

```java
import org.hibernate.annotations.Fetch;
import org.hibernate.annotations.FetchMode;

@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
@Fetch(FetchMode.SUBSELECT)
private List<Item> items;

// FetchMode.SELECT  — default, one query per collection (N+1)
// FetchMode.SUBSELECT — one subselect query for all
// FetchMode.JOIN  — eagerly joins (like FetchType.EAGER but with JOIN)
```

## Production Reality

SUBSELECT is a useful strategy when you consistently load a list of parents and access their collections. It's predictable — always 2 queries. It's less suitable when collections are rarely accessed (you pay for them anyway) or when you need to paginate (the subquery references the original result set which may not have offset/limit). In practice, BATCH with a reasonable size covers most cases well.

## The Error

```
org.hibernate.LazyInitializationException: 
  could not initialize proxy [com.example.User#5] - no Session
```

## Cause 1: Accessing Outside Transaction

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Order findById(Long id) {
        return orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        // Transaction ends, session closes — order.user is still a proxy
    }
}

// In controller or test:
Order order = orderService.findById(1L);  // Session closed!
String userName = order.getUser().getName();  // LazyInitializationException!
```

## Fix 1: Initialize in Transaction

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public OrderDTO findByIdWithUser(Long id) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findByIdWithUser(id).orElseThrow();
    // Map to DTO inside transaction
    return orderMapper.toDTO(order);  // No lazy access after return
}

// Repository:
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.user WHERE o.id = :id")
Optional<Order> findByIdWithUser(@Param("id") Long id);
```

## Fix 2: Hibernate.initialize()

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Order findByIdInitialized(Long id) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    Hibernate.initialize(order.getUser());  // Force-load while session is open
    Hibernate.initialize(order.getItems());
    return order;  // Now safe to use after transaction ends
}
```

## Fix 3: Return DTO Instead of Entity

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public OrderDTO findById(Long id) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // Map ALL needed data to DTO within the transaction
    return new OrderDTO(
        order.getId(),
        order.getUser().getName(),    // Accessed in-transaction — fine
        order.getItems().size()       // Accessed in-transaction — fine
    );
    // DTO returned — no proxies, no LazyInitializationException possible
}
```

## Why OSIV is the Wrong Fix

```properties
# spring.jpa.open-in-view=true (default, but bad)
# Keeps session open until response is sent
# Hides LazyInitializationException — causes N+1 during serialization instead
# Holds DB connection through JSON rendering

# Better:
spring.jpa.open-in-view=false  # Fail fast, fix at the source
```

## Production Reality

Enable `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false` in development immediately. This turns silent N+1 problems (OSIV) into loud `LazyInitializationException` errors that fail fast and tell you exactly where to fix your eager loading strategy. Fixing these properly — with JOIN FETCH or DTO projections — results in a more efficient application than relying on OSIV.

## The Cartesian Product Problem

```sql
-- Fetching order with items and payments simultaneously:
SELECT o.*, i.*, p.*
FROM orders o
JOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.id
JOIN order_payments p ON p.order_id = o.id
WHERE o.id = 1;

-- If order has 3 items and 4 payments:
-- Result: 12 rows (3 × 4)
-- Hibernate must deduplicate
```

## MultipleBagFetchException

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private List<Item> items;      // Bag

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private List<Payment> payments; // Bag
}

// This THROWS: MultipleBagFetchException
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items JOIN FETCH o.payments WHERE o.id = :id")
Optional<Order> findByIdWithAll(@Param("id") Long id);
// Error: cannot simultaneously fetch multiple bags
```

## Solution 1: Use Set

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private Set<Item> items = new HashSet<>();     // Set — not a bag

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private Set<Payment> payments = new HashSet<>();
}

// Now this WORKS (but Cartesian product still happens):
@Query("SELECT DISTINCT o FROM Order o "
     + "JOIN FETCH o.items "
     + "JOIN FETCH o.payments "
     + "WHERE o.id = :id")
Optional<Order> findByIdWithAll(@Param("id") Long id);
```

## Solution 2: Multiple Queries (Often Better)

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public OrderFullDTO getFullOrder(Long id) {
    // Query 1: order with items
    Order order = orderRepo.findByIdWithItems(id).orElseThrow();

    // Query 2: payments separately
    List<Payment> payments = paymentRepo.findByOrderId(id);

    // Map manually
    return orderMapper.toFullDTO(order, payments);
}

// Or in repository:
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.id = :id")
Optional<Order> findByIdWithItems(@Param("id") Long id);

@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.payments WHERE o.id = :id")
Optional<Order> findByIdWithPayments(@Param("id") Long id);

// Load both — Hibernate merges into the same object (same persistence context)
// Total: 2 queries, no Cartesian product
```

## Production Reality

For entities with 2+ large collections, the separate query approach almost always produces better query plans than a Cartesian JOIN. Two targeted queries of 100 rows each is better than one query returning 10,000 rows from a Cartesian product that gets immediately deduplicated. Profile both approaches with your real data before deciding.

## First vs Second Level Cache

```
First-Level Cache (L1):
  - Per Hibernate Session (= per transaction in Spring)
  - Automatic — cannot disable
  - Within one request: same entity loaded twice → one DB query
  - Across requests: cleared — each transaction has empty L1

Second-Level Cache (L2):
  - Shared across Sessions (global, configurable)
  - Must be explicitly enabled per entity
  - Across requests: cached entity survives transaction boundaries
  - Requires a cache provider: Ehcache, Caffeine, Redis, Hazelcast
```

## Enabling Second-Level Cache

```properties
# application.properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.region.factory_class=\
  org.hibernate.cache.jcache.JCacheRegionFactory
# Add Ehcache or Caffeine as JCache provider
```

```java
@Entity
@Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)  // Enable L2 cache
public class Category {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;
    // Mostly read, infrequently updated — good cache candidate
}

// Products referencing Category: N products, but Category loaded from cache
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private Category category;
// After first load: subsequent findById(categoryId) hits L2 cache, not DB
```

## Query Cache

```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_query_cache=true
```

```java
@Repository
public class CategoryRepository extends JpaRepository<Category, Long> {
    @QueryHints({
        @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.cacheable", value = "true"),
        @QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.cacheRegion", value = "category.all")
    })
    @Query("SELECT c FROM Category c WHERE c.active = true")
    List<Category> findAllActive();
    // Same result returned from cache on repeated calls (same parameters)
}
```

## Cache Concurrency Strategies

| Strategy | Use When |
|---|---|
| `READ_ONLY` | Never updated — reference data |
| `READ_WRITE` | Updated, soft lock during update |
| `NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE` | Infrequent updates, stale OK |
| `TRANSACTIONAL` | JTA transactions required |

## Production Reality

Second-level cache is effective for reference/lookup data: categories, countries, currencies, tax rates, configuration values. Avoid caching frequently updated entities — the invalidation overhead and cache misses negate the benefit. In a clustered environment, the cache provider must support distributed invalidation — otherwise instances serve stale data. Ehcache with terracotta or Redis-based caches handle this correctly.
